Unreleased
---
Interface
- WindowAgentWidget::hostWidget() answers which window the agent has taken
  over, or null while it has taken over nothing. setup() answers the same
  question once and in one direction only, and nothing read it back afterwards,
  so an object holding a window could not tell one agent on it from another -
  which is what asking a window material of a window that already draws its own
  caption needs. The answer does not change when a second window is offered:
  an agent takes one, and a refused setup leaves the first in place.

0.3.2
---
Interface
- WindowAgentBase::setBorderOwnsCursor() takes the resize border out of the hit
  test, and borderOwnsCursor() reads it back. A frameless window draws its own
  border and the border it draws is not always there to be grabbed - it may be no
  more than the room a shadow needs, with dragging meant to go through the title
  bar only - and nothing in the geometry says which of the two it is. With the
  flag false a press on the border reaches whatever is drawn there instead of
  starting a resize, and the cursor stays the arrow all the way to the window's
  edge. The title bar keeps its own answer, so dragging by the title bar is
  unaffected. Both backends honour it: the native one at the four places in the
  hit test a dead dontOverrideCursor flag used to sit - two of them in the branch
  a default build takes, with the system borders on - and the portable one through
  the cursor and the edges it hands to startSystemResize(). The answer differs
  either side of the window edge: inside it falls back to the area it sits in, so
  the press reaches what is drawn there, and outside it falls back to a plain
  border, because there is nothing out there to hand a press to and calling it
  client area confuses Windows. Default is true, which is the behaviour every
  window has always had. This is the opt-out whose absence 0.3.0 recorded as a
  limitation; that entry is gone from the limitations page now, and
  tst_qxwindow_native asks a real window procedure for it.

Cleanup
- Drop the Qt 5.14 guard around the private qwinregistry_p.h include in
  windowkit_win.h. The header exists since 5.14, below the 5.15 minimum, so it
  is now included unconditionally.

0.3.1
---
Cleanup
- Unify the internal prefix. The log category is now "qxwindow" (was
  "qwindowkit") and the log / attribute macros in the private headers are
  QXWINDOW_* (was QWK_*), with the cocoa delegate and observer types following.
  Only the category name is observable - use QT_LOGGING_RULES=qxwindow.*=true
  in place of qwindowkit.*. The macros were private to windowkit_p.h and are
  unchanged in effect.

0.3.0
---
Interface
- WindowStyleAgent is removed (it was deprecated in 4.2, K26 / W2). It reported
  the system colour scheme - {Unknown, Light, Dark, HighContrast} - with one
  implementation per platform, while QxThemeManager in qxtheme already answers
  the same question more completely and follows later changes as well. Nothing in
  the tree referred to the class and it was only compiled with
  QX_WINDOW_CONFIG_STYLE_AGENT, an option that goes away with it. HighContrast
  was the one value QxThemeManager does not report and it has no consumer
  anywhere in the tree - that was checked before deleting, and it is also why the
  class does not come back in a smaller "just HighContrast" form: if it is ever
  needed, it is a new requirement. Use QxTheme::QxThemeManager instead; see
  doc/pages/migration.md, section "4.3 已删除"

Cleanup
- Remove the never-assigned dontOverrideCursor flag from the hit test. It was
  written as false and nothing in this library ever set it, so all four of its
  uses already reduced to isFixedSize and the branch it guarded was unreachable.
  Deleting it is an identity transform, so no behaviour changes and no real
  machine check is needed. Wiring up an actual opt-out would be new public API
  and is left to the API review; see the limitations page
- Remove the Qt version branches below 5.15, which K1 set as the minimum: the
  hand-written WindowsRegistryKey class, now an alias for QWinRegistryKey from Qt's
  own private header, and the WindowMoveManipulator / WindowResizeManipulator
  fallbacks in startSystemMove and startSystemResize

Bugfix
- QX_WINDOW_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_WINDOW_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_WINDOW_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version
  being built - (0,2,0) > (0,2,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to false
  on every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays declared
  for as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the cutoff is
  what compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries

Test
- Make the resize border and corner hit test reachable. Which edge or corner a
  press lands on was inline in windowcontext_win.cpp's WM_NCHITTEST handling,
  which is 2000 lines behind a native window; hitTestRegionFor() in
  windowhitregion_p.h is that arithmetic, and the backend calls it rather than
  keeping its own copy. tst_QxWindowHitRegion covers the edges, the corners, the
  widened left and right strips, and what a fixed-size window answers instead.
  The comment on that widened strip was wrong about which strips it applies to,
  and the tests are what showed it: the top and bottom tests use the plain frame
  size, only left and right are doubled
- Cover the system button and title bar hit test with tst_QxWindowHitRegion. The
  two questions windowcontext_win.cpp asks on every WM_NCHITTEST - is this point
  in a system button, is it in the title bar's draggable area - now live in
  windowhitregion_p.h as plain functions over rectangles, and windowcontext.cpp
  calls them rather than keeping its own copy, so the tested code is the code that
  runs. They were untestable before for two reasons at once: the file needs a
  native window to drive, and its symbols are not exported from a Windows DLL
- Cover the Win32 to Qt conversion helpers in windowkit_win_p.h with
  tst_QxWindowWinKit. The whole header is Windows-only - it includes dwmapi.h -
  so the cases sit behind Q_OS_WIN and CI's windows-latest leg is what runs
  them; one case is registered everywhere so the suite keeps its shape. Two
  things the tests pinned that nothing else would have caught:
  margins2qmargins() returns QMargins(1,3,2,4) for MARGINS{1,2,3,4}, because
  MARGINS declares left,right,top,bottom while QMargins declares
  left,top,right,bottom, so the conversion has to move a value rather than copy
  positionally; and the four SIZE ordering operators do not agree with each
  other on equal areas - operator== compares fields while >, >=, < and <=
  compare area, so for two different shapes of the same area "<" is true in
  both directions and ">=" is false in both. Both are recorded as they are
  rather than smoothed over
- Cover getMouseEventScenePos and getMouseEventGlobalPos with tst_QxWindowKit.
  These two exist only because Qt 6 renamed windowPos() to scenePosition() and
  screenPos() to globalPosition(), and both branches compile and both return a
  plausible point, so a test on one Qt major says nothing about the other. The
  cases build an event whose local, scene/window and screen/global values all
  differ, which is what makes reading the wrong field fail. They also pin that
  QPointF::toPoint() rounds rather than truncates, including for negatives where
  the two differ

0.2.0
---
Deprecated
- WindowStyleAgent: deprecated and to be removed. Nothing in the tree refers to
  it, no page documents it, it is only built when QX_WINDOW_CONFIG_STYLE_AGENT is
  on (off by default), and QxThemeManager in qxtheme already follows the system
  colour scheme - more completely than this class does. HighContrast is the one
  value QxThemeManager does not report; if that becomes a real need, revisit it
  then.

Cleanup
- Settle the eight TODO/FIXME comments inherited from upstream (seven in
  windowcontext_win.cpp, one in windowsystem_p.h). Two of them were dead `#if 0`
  blocks that only listed WM_NCPOINTER* cases and are now removed; the remaining
  four sites became NOTE comments, each pointing at an entry in
  doc/pages/limitations.md. No behaviour change and no API change.
- Move the resize border geometry out of windowcontext_qt.cpp into
  windowgeometry_p.h, as plain functions over a QSize. It was two file-static
  helpers taking a QWindow* only to read width(), height() and visibility(); the
  first two are the whole calculation, so they are now reachable without a
  window and the backend keeps a thin wrapper with the visibility guard. No
  behaviour change and no API change - the point is that the arithmetic
  (which edges a point resizes from, which cursor advertises them) is covered by
  tests/qxwindow/auto/tst_qxwindowgeometry.cpp instead of being untested.

